window.docContents[245].resolve({"/Write-Once___-Correctly/Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum--CPU___-multi-threaded-___-coarse-grained-task-parallelism":{"contents":"Keep the scalar carrier and split the row-independent (pixel) axis into contiguous slices, running\nthe whole kernel on each slice on its own dedicated Lean Task. The parallelism is Lean's own task\nconstruct — no external threading library, no OpenMP shim — and it is coarse-grained: one task per\nslice runs the entire op-chain, so the compute-per-fork is maximal and per-operation fork/join\noverhead is nil. This is exact whenever the work-items are independent (which the per-pixel retrieval\nis), and it scales toward the core count when the model has good arithmetic intensity — enough\ncompute per work-item that the cores are kept busy rather than starved on memory bandwidth or throttled\nby task overhead. A model with poor intensity, or one whose items are not independent, sees little\nbenefit here and is better served by the single-threaded baseline. This strategy is the deployment\nlayer's runSharded.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Write Once, Correctly\u0009Go fast, automatically — the deployment spectrum","header":"15.3.2. CPU, multi-threaded — coarse-grained task parallelism","id":"/Write-Once___-Correctly/Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum--CPU___-multi-threaded-___-coarse-grained-task-parallelism"}});